  "final_text": "Both the dashboard and the alert rule now exist, and the rule is enabled.\n\n**Dashboard: \"EFF-69b95057 Web logs overview\"**\n- URL: http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-69b95057-web-logs-overview-cc2bdd2197bc\n- Five panels on the `Kibana Sample Data Logs` view:\n  - Total requests (count)\n  - Unique client IPs (unique count of `clientip`)\n  - Requests over time (line, date histogram on `@timestamp`)\n  - Requests by response code (pie, terms on `response.keyword`)\n  - Top destination countries (bar, terms on `geo.dest`)\n- Time range: last 1 year. I picked this because I couldn't check the sample data's dates.\n\n**Alert rule: \"EFF-69b95057 5xx spike\"**\n- ID: `2aec2a46-84dd-4701-9bdf-9773af8ac0dd`, type `.es-query`, consumer `stackAlerts`\n- Runs every 1m. Searches `kibana_sample_data_logs` over a 5-minute window on `timestamp`, and fires when the hit count is greater than 10.\n- The query is a wildcard match on `response.keyword` for `5*`, which covers 500\u2013599.\n- Enabled. Its status reads `ok` after enabling.\n\n**What I couldn't verify**\n- I didn't check the response code values. Raw ES|QL queries failed: the Kibana console proxy dropped the API key, and the `/api/esql/query` route doesn't exist on this instance. So the `5*` wildcard assumes the codes are stored as strings like `\"503\"`, which the `response` field type suggests but I didn't confirm. The response-code pie chart will show the real values.\n- I didn't open the dashboard in a browser, so I haven't confirmed the panels render data.\n- The rule may not fire on this data. If the sample timestamps are older than the last 5 minutes, the window will be empty.",
